# cobra 0.31.1, numpy 1.26.4 -- reading, gapfilling and solving genome-scale models.
#
# The image is `docker/cobra/`, built and verified by `docker/cobra/dev.sh --build
# --check` and PUSHED to quay as quay.io/hallamlab/cobra:0.31.1
# (sha256:ae2d28f29167f2b17fdb2e7820af727f5021c9ab3c6355e1601df947038e764e,
# 2026-09-05). Its verification program builds a two-reaction model and asserts the LP
# objective, so a broken solver fails at build rather than in a run.
#
# THE `container:` LINE IS STILL COMMENTED OUT, and this is the last step: quay creates
# a new repository PRIVATE, and hallamlab/cobra is still private, so an anonymous pull
# 401s. Naming it here now points every solve at an image the drawing host cannot see.
# Flip the repository to public at
# https://quay.io/repository/hallamlab/cobra?tab=settings -- the changevisibility API
# needs an OAuth token, which a docker push credential is not -- then uncomment the line
# below and delete this paragraph. Verify first:
#   curl -sI -H "Authorization: Bearer $(curl -s \
#     'https://quay.io/v2/auth?service=quay.io&scope=repository:hallamlab/cobra:pull' \
#     | python3 -c 'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["token"])')" \
#     https://quay.io/v2/hallamlab/cobra/manifests/0.31.1
# 200 means public, 401 means it is not.
#
# `quay.io/biocontainers/cobra` was checked first and is not a substitute: it IS cobrapy
# and its LP solver works, but 0.29.1 (its newest, December 2024 -- the package moved to
# conda-forge) carries neither `requests` nor `scipy`, and `lib::modelling` fetches BiGG
# models with requests.
#
# All four kbase modelling transforms were re-run against THIS pushed image in curation
# round 6 and reproduce round 5's numbers exactly: cobra_fba 0.8739 on minimal glucose,
# infeasible with the carbon source shut, the gpmA knockout neutral; fetch_bigg_model
# 95/72/137 for e_coli_core; gem_from_gpr 15 reactions from 15 MNXR; cobra_gapfill 0.0
# to 0.639 by adding two reactions from a 66-reaction universe.
# See research/kbase/curation/r5/runs.md for what each check means.
#
# numpy<2 is why this cannot merge into `python_for_data_science`: cobra's optlang stack
# needs it and polars needs numpy>=2.
# container: docker://quay.io/hallamlab/cobra:0.31.1
conda: build-refs-cobra
